// =========================================================================
// dlcd-interop.spthy — the says-credential ↔ Biscuit/RFC-8707 interop protocol
// =========================================================================
//
// Phase 2 of the agent-governance program (spec/interop-says-biscuit.md). A
// DLC-D `says`-credential (`Term::Sign : Says(p, φ)`, realized by Ed25519 in
// `dlc-crypto::signed_term`) is the same object a Biscuit root block encodes:
// an issuer vouches, under its key, for a capability bound to a resource
// AUDIENCE (RFC 8707 `resource`/`aud`, mirrored by commit-I's sink/label `ℓ`).
// This theory models minting + audience-bound verification and proves the two
// safety properties an interop bridge must preserve, plus a non-vacuity witness.
//
// MODEL-FIRST (CLAUDE.md): no `dlc-interop` wire code exists until this model
// does. Mirrors the house style of `dlcd-replication.spthy` (builtins: signing;
// the Equality restriction discharges signature checks; long-term-key reveal
// conditions every all-traces lemma; an exists-trace witness rules out vacuity).
//
// SCOPE OF THIS INCREMENT: root issuance + audience binding. Offline ATTENUATION
// (Biscuit's append-a-narrowing-block delegation) and its monotonicity lemma
// (`attenuation never widens`) + the differential BITE are the next increment —
// stated as fences below, not yet modelled.
//
// Modelling choices:
//   1. A credential is `<'cred', I, aud, scope, sign(<'cred', I, aud, scope>, skI)>`
//      — issuer `I`, audience `aud`, a fresh `scope` (the granted right), signed
//      by `I`. This is the wire form of `Says(I, cap@aud)`.
//   2. `scope` is `Fr(~scope)` — fresh per issuance — so a scope value names
//      exactly one issuance. That is what makes `no_cross_audience_replay`
//      provable without modelling scope contents.
//   3. Verification is audience-bound: a resource server for `$srvAud` accepts a
//      credential only if the signature verifies AND `credAud = $srvAud`.

theory DLCD_Interop
begin

builtins: signing

// =========================================================================
// Restrictions
// =========================================================================

// Signature verification, the derivable way: the Verify rule emits the check as
// an action and this restriction discharges it (same idiom as the replication
// model's `Equality`).
restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

// One keypair per principal — otherwise "its" signature is not well defined.
restriction unique_key:
  "All A #i #j.
      KeyRegistered(A) @ #i & KeyRegistered(A) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

// =========================================================================
// Keys and compromise
// =========================================================================

rule Register_key:
    [ Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ HonestKey($A, ~sk), KeyRegistered($A) ]->
    [ !Ltk($A, ~sk), !Pk($A, pk(~sk)), Out(pk(~sk)) ]

// Static long-term-key reveal — the adversary learns a principal's secret.
// Every all-traces lemma below is conditioned on this not having happened for
// the issuer it names.
rule Reveal_ltk:
    [ !Ltk($A, sk) ]
  --[ Reveal($A) ]->
    [ Out(sk) ]

// =========================================================================
// The protocol
// =========================================================================

// An issuer mints a says-credential for a capability bound to an audience: the
// Biscuit root block / RFC-8707-audience-bound token. `scope` is the granted
// right (fresh; contents abstracted).
rule Issue_cred:
    [ Fr(~scope), !Ltk($I, skI) ]
  --[ CredIssued($I, $aud, ~scope) ]->
    [ Out(<'cred', $I, $aud, ~scope, sign(<'cred', $I, $aud, ~scope>, skI)>) ]

// A resource server bound to audience `$srvAud` verifies and accepts a
// credential. It checks (a) the issuer's signature over the exact credential
// body, and (b) that the credential's audience equals its own — the RFC-8707
// audience binding that defeats cross-resource replay.
rule Verify_at_audience:
    [ In(<'cred', I, credAud, scope, sig>), !Pk(I, pkI) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, <'cred', I, credAud, scope>, pkI), true)
    , Eq(credAud, $srvAud)
    , Accepted(I, $srvAud, scope) ]->
    [ ]

// =========================================================================
// Security lemmas (all-traces) + non-vacuity witness (exists-trace)
// =========================================================================

// FORGERY-RESISTANCE. An accepted credential was really issued by the issuer it
// names — unless that issuer's key was revealed (Dolev–Yao). This is the wire
// form of what `dlc-crypto::signed_term::verify_in_keyring` buys: acceptance
// implies a genuine `Says(I, …)` by `I`.
lemma cred_binds_issuer:
  all-traces
  "All I aud sc #i.
       Accepted(I, aud, sc) @ #i
    ==>
       (Ex #j. CredIssued(I, aud, sc) @ #j)
     | (Ex #r. Reveal(I) @ #r)"

// AUDIENCE-BINDING (RFC 8707). A credential issued for one audience is never
// accepted at a different one: no cross-audience / cross-resource replay. (A
// fresh `scope` names one issuance, so equal scope forces equal audience.)
lemma no_cross_audience_replay:
  all-traces
  "All I aud1 aud2 sc #i #j.
       CredIssued(I, aud1, sc) @ #j & Accepted(I, aud2, sc) @ #i
    ==>
       aud1 = aud2
     | (Ex #r. Reveal(I) @ #r)"

// NON-VACUITY (anti-vacuity witness). The safety lemmas above are not vacuously
// true: there is a trace where an honest credential is issued and accepted at
// its audience with no key ever revealed. Without this, `cred_binds_issuer`
// could hold simply because nothing is ever accepted.
lemma exec_accept:
  exists-trace
  "Ex I aud sc #i #j.
       CredIssued(I, aud, sc) @ #j
     & Accepted(I, aud, sc) @ #i
     & not (Ex X #r. Reveal(X) @ #r)"

// =========================================================================
// Fences (next increments — NOT yet modelled)
// =========================================================================
//   * ATTENUATION-MONOTONE: an `Attenuate` rule appends a narrowing block
//     (Biscuit offline attenuation), and a lemma that an accepted attenuated
//     token never grants MORE than its root — the attenuate-only invariant that
//     `Delegate`/`Attenuate`/`SpeaksFor` (RFC 8693) must preserve.
//   * The differential BITE (`dlcd-interop-bite.spthy` + check-tamarin-bite):
//     removing the audience-equality check must make `no_cross_audience_replay`
//     FALSIFIABLE — the machine-checked statement that the check is load-bearing.
//   * ProVerif cross-check (`models/proverif/dlcd-interop.pv`).

end
